---
// Lesson 3.1a — The Turret: Distance & the Detection Radius
// Vector3.Distance, maxAttackRange, the boundary case.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>3.1a — The Turret: Distance &amp; the Detection Radius</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The turret is your first adversary that <em>decides</em>. Its whole
    brain, today, is one number: how far away are you? One if-statement
    separates "asleep" from "shooting." Every boss you build later starts
    from this exact idea.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Predict First</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Read TurretController.cs — the turret's awareness, in full:</p>
    <pre><code>[SerializeField] private float maxAttackRange = 7f;
...
private void Update()
{
    float distanceToPlayer = Vector3.Distance(transform.position, playerController.transform.position);

    if (distanceToPlayer &lt;= maxAttackRange)
    {
        // (today: just watch the boundary; the shooting comes in 3.2a)
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p>
        Predict before running: you creep toward the turret. At what
        distance does it "see" you — exactly 7, or 6.99, or 7.01? What
        happens when you stand exactly on the boundary and jitter one
        pixel in and out? Write your prediction; the Debug.Log in the
        lab will settle it.
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            Vector3.Distance is a function someone else wrote — square
            roots inside, probably. You call it without ever seeing the
            math. What is that hiding called on the AP exam, and what
            would your movement code from 1.4a look like if you had to
            write Translate from raw matrix math every time?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — place a turret and read its knob.</strong> Drag
    the Turret prefab into the Overworld, select it, and find
    <strong>Max Attack Range</strong> in the Inspector: 7. The turret's
    brain is a serialized float, exactly like your xSpeed. Everything
    about its difficulty lives in tunable numbers.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — see the number live.</strong> Add this to the
    turret's Update for today (delete it after — it is a measurement,
    not a feature):</p>
    <pre><code>Debug.Log(distanceToPlayer);</code></pre>

    <p>
        Walk toward the turret and watch the Console. Stop at the moment
        the number crosses 7.0 — did anything visible change? (Not yet:
        the shooting fires nothing today. You are watching the
        <em>condition</em>, in the raw.)
    </p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — the boundary experiment.</strong> Stand at the
    edge: the condition is <code>&lt;=</code>, so 7.0 counts. Now
    change it to <code>&lt;</code> and stand exactly at 7.0 (use the
    Inspector readout of the player's transform to get surgical). What
    does &lt;= vs &lt; change about the game? One pixel of fairness —
    the same trap the AP exam tests with list indexes.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The blind turret</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Set maxAttackRange to 0.5 and stand away. Why does
                "shrinking the radius" produce the same behavior as
                deleting the turret — and what is the design lesson?</li>
            <li>Move the player out of the scene (delete the Player
                GameObject from the Hierarchy) and press Play. What
                does the Console say, and which line of Start() wrote
                that message?</li>
            <li>Comment out the Distance call and hardcode
                <code>float distanceToPlayer = 3f;</code>. What did
                you just build — a turret that always shoots, or one
                that never does?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Design the aggro</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Tune maxAttackRange by playtesting: what feels right —
                5? 9? 12? Where does "scary" become "unfair"? Write your
                winner and one sentence why.</li>
            <li>Design: should the turret keep its detection radius a
                secret from the player, or telegraph it? How would a
                designer SHOW a 7-unit radius without printing numbers?</li>
            <li>Hard: could you build a turret that only sees you in
                one direction (a cone)? What other function would you
                need besides distance? Sketch the idea; you will
                actually build it in the boss unit.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain what Vector3.Distance returns and use it in a condition</li>
            <li>Predict the &lt;= boundary case and verify it live</li>
            <li>Explain why the turret finds the player by tag in Start, not Update</li>
            <li>Tune maxAttackRange like a designer, with evidence</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/index.html">Unit 3: The Turret</a> — distance, radius, projectile lifecycle</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — Vector3.Distance and friends</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="3.2a.html">3.2a — Projectiles: Instantiate, Fire, Destroy</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="3.1a" title="3.1a The Turret: Distance &amp; the Detection Radius — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
